'''''Cerastium diffusum''''', the '''fourstamen chickweed'''<ref>{{PLANTS|id=CEDI7|taxon=Cerastium diffusum|accessdate=18 June 2016}}</ref> or '''sea mouse-ear''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=18 June 2016 |df=dmy-all}}</ref> is a species of flowering plant in the pink and carnation family [[Caryophyllaceae]]. It is an annual [[herb]], up to 30&nbsp;cm.high, occurring in western Europe and northern Africa. It is mainly found in coastal areas of Algeria, the Baleares, Belgium, Corsica, Denmark, France, the Faroe Islands, Germany, Great Britain, Ireland, Italy, Libya, Morocco, Netherlands, Norway, Portugal, Sardinia, Sicily, Spain and Sweden.<ref name="POWO">{{cite web |title=''Cerastium diffusum'' Pers. |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:152210-1 |website=Plants of the World Online |access-date=15 April 2022 |language=en|publisher=Kew Science}}</ref> The flowers have 4 (or rarely 5) petals, 4 or 5 [[stamen]]s appearing between March and May. The [[petal]]s are much shorter than the [[sepal]]s. The leaves are opposite, (sessile) without petioles and the sepals and bracts are all green, without pale margins. The fruit petioles are upright and diffuse at maturity.<ref name=Stace>{{cite book|last=Stace|first=C. A.|authorlink = Stace, C. A.|year=2019|title=New Flora of the British Isles|edition=Fourth|publisher=C & M Floristics|location = Middlewood Green, Suffolk, U.K.| isbn=978-1-5272-2630-2}}</ref>{{rp|490}}<ref name=Blamey>{{cite book|last1=Blamey|first1=M.|last2=Fitter|first2=R.|last3=Fitter|first3=A|year=2003|title=Wild flowers of Britain and Ireland: The Complete Guide to the British and Irish Flora.|publisher=A & C Black|location=London|isbn=978-1408179505}}</ref>{{rp|52}}

There are two known [[Infraspecific name|Infraspecifics]]:<ref name="POWO"/>
* ''Cerastium diffusum'' subsp. ''diffusum''
* ''Cerastium diffusum'' subsp. ''gussonei'' {{small|(Tod. ex Lojac.) P.D.Sell & Whitehead}}

It was first published in Synopsis plantarum 1 on page 520 in 1805.<ref name="POWO"/>

